3. (removed) TLS support -- was LGPL-2.1-or-later
-------------------------------------------------
    This project used to carry SSLEngineManager.java (LGPL-2.1-or-later, by
    Brian P. Hinz and D. R. Commander) together with rdr/TLSInStream.java,
    rdr/TLSOutStream.java and rdr/TLSException.java.

    They were dead code: SecurityClient offers only None and VncAuth, so no TLS
    security type is ever negotiated and nothing ever reached them.  They have
    been deleted, and with them the only LGPL-licensed file in the project.

    If TLS is ever added back, take the files from upstream TigerVNC again and
    restore an LGPL-2.1 notice here along with the license text.

    (The TLS/VeNCrypt constants that remain in rfb/Security.java are just RFB
    protocol numbers, not an implementation.)


4. libjpeg-turbo -- IJG License AND BSD-3-Clause (plus zlib, BSD-2-Clause)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Source:   https://github.com/libjpeg-turbo/libjpeg-turbo
    Revision: 7b5fee3f7a83b5c2bdbb52d5dd6ce6bfc53b6b58
              = 3.2.0-29-g7b5fee3, dated 2026-09-11.  A master snapshot, not a
              tagged release.  (The ChangeLog's "3.2.1" section is the
              in-development one; 3.2.1 was not released at this revision.)
    In tree:  app/src/main/cpp/libjpeg-turbo/  -- a git SUBMODULE, so the code
              is fetched from upstream, not redistributed here.

        git submodule update --init --recursive

    License text: app/src/main/cpp/libjpeg-turbo/LICENSE.md  (in the submodule)
    IJG readme:   app/src/main/cpp/libjpeg-turbo/README.ijg  (in the submodule)

    The IJG License requires that README.ijg be included in the distribution
    and that its text not be altered.  Both ship inside the submodule at the
    pinned revision; do not remove them.

    NOT ONE BYTE IS CHANGED.  The submodule is pinned to a pristine upstream
    commit and carries no patch.  An earlier version of this project patched
    libjpeg-turbo's CMakeLists.txt to defeat its deliberate block on
    add_subdirectory() integration; that made a submodule impossible, because
    a fresh clone would get clean upstream and fail to build.  The build now
    uses ExternalProject_Add() instead, which is what upstream tells downstream
    projects to do.  See app/src/main/cpp/CMakeLists.txt.


